# Bubble sort

Walk the array comparing each adjacent pair, swapping them when they are out of
order. Each full pass carries the largest remaining element to its final
position — it *bubbles* to the end — so after $k$ passes the last $k$ elements
are sorted.

**Invariant.** After pass $k$, the final $k$ elements are in their sorted
positions and are each at least as large as everything before them.

## Flow

```d2
direction: down

start: Start {shape: oval}
outer: "for i in 0..n-1" {shape: hexagon}
inner: "for j in 0..n-i-2" {shape: hexagon}
compare: "a[j] > a[j+1]?" {shape: diamond}
swap: "swap a[j], a[j+1]"
mark: "swapped = true"
check: "any swaps this pass?" {shape: diamond}
done: Sorted {shape: oval}

start -> outer
outer -> inner
inner -> compare
compare -> swap: yes
compare -> inner: no
swap -> mark
mark -> inner
inner -> check: "pass complete"
check -> outer: yes
check -> done: "no — already sorted"
```

## Cost

- **Best** $O(n)$ — one pass with no swaps, which the early-exit check detects.
- **Average and worst** $O(n^2)$ — roughly $n^2/2$ comparisons and $n^2/4$ swaps.
- **Space** $O(1)$. In-place, and [[stability|stable]].

The swap count is what hurts. Bubble sort makes one swap per inversion, and a
reversed array has $\binom{n}{2}$ of them:

$$
\text{swaps}_{\text{worst}} = \frac{n(n-1)}{2}
$$

!!! warning "Not a practical sort"

    [[insertion-sort]] has the same asymptotic cost and the same invariant
    shape, but does about half the writes, because it shifts a run once instead
    of swapping repeatedly. Bubble sort survives as a teaching device, not as a
    tool. If you reach for it in real code, reach for insertion sort instead.

## Why it is stable

A swap happens only when `a[j] > a[j+1]` — strictly greater. Equal neighbours
are never exchanged, so equal elements keep their relative order.

